  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure will remain in control with dry conditions and\nabove average daytime high temperatures through at least\nSaturday. \n\nThe next chance for rain isn't expected until late Sunday, but \nthere is still a lot of uncertainty with the synoptic setup this\nfar out. Although, latest model trends are delaying the cold\nfront pushing in from the west, allowing the coastal low to\ntrack further west along the coast or even inland. Best chance \nfor widespread rain at this time is from the I81 corridor on \neastward. If this system can track a little further north and \nwest, then it would bring a much needed ample rainfall event to \nour region. Conditions have been quite dry this fall season, \nwith the Finger Lakes Region now in a moderate drought, so any \nrain will be beneficial at this point. \n\nThere is still plenty of time for the forecast guidance to \ncontinue to adjust the exact track and timing of this system.\nThe 18Z & 00Z runs of the operational Euro have come into \nbetter agreement with the GFS and Canadian for the more westerly\ntrack. Most guidance brings the western shield of rainfall into\nthe area by the mid-evening hours on Sunday with showers\ncontinuing into Monday morning. Just how long showers last will\nultimately depend on the track of the system. \n\nBeyond Sunday, there is way too much uncertainty in the long\nterm forecast at this time. High pressure is looking likely for\nthe first half of the week, however the pattern may get a little\nmore interesting by Thanksgiving Day and towards the end of the\nweek. This will be something to watch in the coming days, but\nfor now, confidence is way too low to latch onto any one\nsolution. The most noticeable change for next week will come\nwith the cooler temperatures behind the passing cold front on\nMonday. By the middle of next week temperatures will be around \n20 degrees cooler than the well above normal values we've been\nexperiencing.",
